(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-1-cam-nutrition-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"CAM/Nutrition Exam 1 — Master Exam (Form E)","category":"CAM / Nutrition","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Hindmilk, released later in a feeding, differs from foremilk in that it:","choices":["Contains more water and resembles skim milk","Is only produced during the colostrum stage","Is released within the first 3 to 5 minutes of feeding","Resembles cream, being released later in the feeding"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture states hindmilk, released after about 10 to 20 minutes of feeding, resembles cream. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"According to the lecture, what training does a Registered Dietitian complete?","choices":["A weekend certification course only","A medical degree and residency in surgery","No formal training or credentialing","A four-year degree, usually a post-graduate internship, and a national exam through the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: a Registered Dietitian completes a four-year degree, typically an internship, and the national Registered Dietitian exam. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 61"},{"q":"What percentage of total daily energy needs does basal metabolism (BMR) account for, per lecture?","choices":["8% to 15%","Nearly 100%","15% to 30%","60% to 80%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Basal metabolism accounts for approximately 60% to 80% of total daily energy needs. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Per the slide's list of cardiovascular disease risk factors, an HDL level below what value is considered a risk factor?","choices":["60 mg per dL","40 mg per dL","200 mg per dL","100 mg per dL"],"answer":1,"exp":"Low HDL — under 40 mg per dL — is listed as a cardiovascular risk factor on the slide, alongside total cholesterol over 200 mg/dL and triglycerides over 200 mg/dL. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 69 (Risk Factors for Cardiovascular Disease)"},{"q":"Once breastfeeding is well established, a well-nourished infant's stool should typically appear:","choices":["Chalky and firm","Lumpy, mustard-appearing","Watery and clear","Thick and black (meconium) beyond the first week"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture states a well-nourished breastfed infant should have lumpy, mustard-appearing stool. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"The lecture states about what fraction of school children do not eat breakfast, missing nutrients that fuel the brain and body?","choices":["About 1 in 100","About 90%","Almost none","About one-third"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: as many as one-third of school children do not eat breakfast, worsening into the teenage years. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 483"},{"q":"According to the lecture, how much calcium per day do 9-to-18-year-old boys and girls need?","choices":["1,300 milligrams per day","700 milligrams per day","500 milligrams per day","2,500 milligrams per day"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: teenagers ages 9 to 18 need 1,300 milligrams of calcium per day. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 535"},{"q":"How does the lecture distinguish nutrient density from energy density?","choices":["Nutrient density compares a food's nutrient content to its calories; energy density compares a food's calorie content to its weight","Both terms mean the total calories in a serving","Nutrient density is calories per gram and energy density is nutrients per gram","Energy density measures the vitamins in a food"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: nutrient density is nutrients per calorie, while energy density is calories per weight of food. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 51"},{"q":"Which statement about vitamin content in fruits and vegetables, a key food source of many vitamins, is accurate according to the lecture?","choices":["Vitamin content is greatest when a fruit or vegetable is ripe","Freezing destroys nearly all vitamin content","Water-soluble vitamins are resistant to destruction by heat and light","Vitamin content is greatest when a fruit or vegetable is unripe"],"answer":0,"exp":"Vitamin content is greatest when a fruit or vegetable is ripe, and freezing helps retain nutrients since frozen foods are often blanched first, preserving vitamin content. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 105 (Vitamin Preservation in Foods)"},{"q":"The lecture notes the thrifty phenotype was historically advantageous but is less beneficial today for what reason?","choices":["In a sedentary society it is prone to weight gain","It now requires far more calories than others","It prevents the body from storing any fat","It burns energy too rapidly during fasting"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: because it stores fat easily, the thrifty phenotype is prone to weight gain in a sedentary society. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"The lecture lists signs an infant is satiated during feeding. Which set matches?","choices":["Turning the head away, falling asleep, or becoming playful","Crying loudly and rooting","Clenching fists and stiffening","Sucking faster on the bottle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: signs of satiety include turning the head away, falling asleep, or becoming playful. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 205"},{"q":"Which of the following does the lecture list as a toxic food contaminant capable of causing food intolerance-type reactions?","choices":["Lactase deficiency","Monosodium glutamate (MSG)","Sulfites","Clostridium botulinum"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the lecture lists Clostridium botulinum as a 'toxic contaminant' under food contaminants. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which body-fat measurement method is described as most accurate, but expensive, with minimal radiation dose?","choices":["Dual X-ray photon absorptiometry (DXA)","Skinfold measurement using handheld calipers","Underwater (hydrostatic) weighing","Bioelectrical impedance analysis"],"answer":0,"exp":"DXA is described as the most accurate method, though expensive, with a minimal radiation dose — the scanner arm moves from head to toe to determine body fat and bone density. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed among the malnutrition risk factors present BEFORE hospital admission?","choices":["Worsening chronic illness","Need for mealtime assistance","NPO status for a procedure","Polypharmacy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Polypharmacy is listed among the \"before admit\" risk factors, along with older age, nursing home residence, chronic illness, chronic alcohol use, food insecurity, homelessness, gastric bypass history, and mental health conditions. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"The lecture links social determinants of health to \"health literacy,\" which it defines as the ability to do what?","choices":["Find, understand, and use reliable health information and services to make informed decisions","Memorise the exact calorie content of all of the most common everyday foods","Prescribe one's own medications safely","Perform laboratory nutrition testing at home"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: health literacy is the ability to find, understand, and use reliable health information and services. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What does indirect calorimetry measure, per lecture?","choices":["Skinfold thickness measured with calipers at multiple standardized body sites","Oxygen intake and carbon dioxide output, used to estimate energy expenditure","Waist circumference measured precisely at the level of the umbilicus","Body heat output directly, via an insulated chamber"],"answer":1,"exp":"Indirect calorimetry measures oxygen intake and carbon dioxide output as a surrogate for the substrates consumed and produced during metabolism, estimating energy expenditure. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A fasting blood glucose level is classified as hyperglycemia when it exceeds approximately what value?","choices":["70 mg per 100 mL of blood","180 mg per 100 mL of blood","125 mg per 100 mL of blood","100 mg per 100 mL of blood"],"answer":2,"exp":"Per the slide, hyperglycemia is defined as fasting blood glucose greater than 125 milligrams per 100 milliliters of blood. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 32 (Regulating Blood Glucose)"},{"q":"According to the lecture, approximately what percentage of the U.S. population is affected by celiac disease, and how is it treated?","choices":["10 to 15%; treated with a low-FODMAP dietary pattern","Approximately 1%; treated by eliminating wheat, rye, and barley","About 20%; treated with proton pump inhibitors","About 50%; treated with a course of antibiotics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states celiac disease affects approximately 1% of the U.S. population and is treated by elimination of wheat, rye, and barley. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why doesn't a normal BMI rule out malnutrition, per lecture?","choices":["BMI is never actually measured or documented for malnourished patients admitted to the hospital.","Many malnourished patients have normal or above-normal BMIs due to residual obesity or an expanded extracellular fluid volume.","Malnutrition invariably causes BMI to rise well above the normal reference range in every patient.","BMI calculations are only considered clinically valid for pediatric and adolescent patient populations."],"answer":1,"exp":"Many patients with malnutrition have normal or above-normal BMIs because of residual obesity or expanded extracellular fluid volume (e.g. edema), so BMI alone can mask malnutrition. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What nutrition goal applies to cancer patients undergoing treatment, per lecture?","choices":["Restrict calories to below basal needs, since lower intake is believed to slow tumor growth.","Avoid weight loss with adequate calories/protein, small frequent meals, and symptom management.","Encourage gradual weight loss throughout treatment to reduce chemotherapy side effects.","Avoid all oral nutrition supplements in favor of whole-food meals exclusively."],"answer":1,"exp":"The goal is to avoid weight loss during cancer treatment through adequate calories and protein, small frequent meals, and managing symptoms like nausea, mucositis, and taste changes. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What are the \"3 Ms\" of weight-loss maintenance and relapse prevention, per lecture?","choices":["Meal-planning, Mindfulness, Maintenance","Medication, Meals, Metabolism","Measurement, Moderation, Motivation","Motivation, Movement, and Monitoring"],"answer":3,"exp":"The \"3 Ms\" of weight-loss maintenance are Motivation, Movement, and Monitoring. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"According to the lecture, regular exercise can do what to age-related reductions in lean body mass and calorie needs?","choices":["Permanently stop all aging","Halt, slow, and even reverse reductions in lean body mass and halt the decline in calorie needs","Accelerate the loss of lean body mass and speed the decline in daily calorie needs","Have no effect on lean body mass"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: exercise can halt, slow, and even reverse lean-mass loss and halt the decline in calorie needs. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What does \"stimulus control\" refer to as a behavioral weight-loss strategy, per lecture?","choices":["A surgical technique used to physically reduce overall stomach volume.","Taking prescription medication that directly suppresses the appetite stimulus.","Putting a person in charge of managing temptations in their environment.","A method of automatically counting and logging calories through a smartphone app."],"answer":2,"exp":"Stimulus control puts a person in charge of their temptations, such as storing food out of sight or avoiding buffet-style meals. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What protein intake does the lecture recommend for older adults to help preserve muscle and bone mass?","choices":["1.0 to 1.2 grams per kilogram per day","0.4 to 0.6 grams per kilogram per day","2.5 to 3.0 grams per kilogram per day","No specific amount is recommended"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the recommended intake is 1.0 to 1.2 grams of protein per kilogram per day. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"According to the lecture, what share of the calories in breast milk is supplied by protein?","choices":["About 35% to 40%","About 75%","Less than 10%","About 55%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: protein supplies less than 10% of the total calories in breast milk. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 180"},{"q":"The lecture recommends enhancing insulin use and restoring normal blood glucose in older adults through which measures?","choices":["Avoiding all physical activity","Eating a high-sugar, low-fiber diet","Maintaining a healthy weight, regular activity, and a low-fat, high-fiber diet with fewer processed foods","Increasing the intake of processed foods, refined sugar, and saturated fat while resting more"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: these measures are recommended to enhance insulin use and restore normal blood glucose. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"The lecture describes Healthy People 2030 as a set of national health objectives published on what schedule?","choices":["Every year","Every 2 years","Every 10 years","Every 5 years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Healthy People objectives are published every 10 years. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 35"},{"q":"A pregnant patient reports nausea and vomiting. Which dietary/supplement strategy does the lecture recommend specifically for this discomfort?","choices":["Pyridoxine (vitamin B6), with or without doxylamine, ginger, and eating cold foods sparingly but often.","Increasing both the fluid intake and the dietary fibre consumption throughout the day.","Avoiding acidic foods and not eating close to bedtime each night.","Avoiding caffeine entirely, limiting intake to under 200 mg per day."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists pyridoxine with or without doxylamine, ginger, avoiding strong smells/spicy foods, cold foods, and eating sparingly but often as remedies for nausea and vomiting. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"The lecture notes carotenoids such as lutein and zeaxanthin are linked to prevention of which conditions?","choices":["Cataracts and macular degeneration","Osteoporosis and hip fractures","Anemia and fatigue","Constipation and reflux"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: lutein and zeaxanthin are linked to prevention of cataracts and macular degeneration. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What effect does celiac disease have on the small intestine, according to the lecture?","choices":["It creates diverticula along the intestinal lining.","It causes ulceration within the duodenal and gastric lining.","It thickens the intestinal wall, slowing motility.","It flattens the villi, limiting nutrient absorption."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the lecture states celiac disease 'flattens villi' and 'limits absorption of nutrients.' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"According to the lecture, what fat-intake range is appropriate for children ages 1 to 3 years?","choices":["5% to 10% of calories","30% to 40% of calories","10% to 15% of calories","50% to 60% of calories"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: children 1 to 3 years old have a recommended fat-intake range of 30% to 40% of calories. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"The lecture notes that increased affluence in North America has been linked to which dietary and lifestyle shift?","choices":["A universal, exclusive improvement in nutrient density seen equally across every income level","Higher intake of animal fat, sugar, salt, and alcohol, alongside more sedentary lifestyles","A complete shift away from convenience and processed foods toward home-cooked, whole-food meals","Reduced intake of animal fat, sugar, salt, and alcohol across nearly every demographic group"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lecture states that although North Americans generally live longer and have more money, diverse food, and lifestyle choices, affluence has also led to sedentary lifestyles and high intakes of animal fat, sugar, salt, and alcohol. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 42 (Longer Healthier Lives)"},{"q":"Per the lecture, adolescent pregnancy (under age 20) carries an increased risk for which of the following outcomes?","choices":["Preeclampsia-eclampsia, prematurity, and small for gestational age infants","Fetal aneuploidy and gestational diabetes risk","Macrosomia and increased cesarean delivery rate","Obstructive lung disease appearing later in the offspring"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture lists these as increased risks associated with adolescent (under 20) pregnancy. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed as part of the 'history' component of food allergy diagnosis in the lecture?","choices":["Genetic testing results from a specialty laboratory panel","The patient's typical weekly exercise frequency","The quantity of food required to produce a reaction","The patient's documented blood type on file"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the lecture's history component includes 'quantity of food required to produce reaction' among other details. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which food source provides the long-chain omega-3 fatty acids DHA and EPA directly, rather than requiring the body to convert a shorter-chain precursor?","choices":["Fatty fish such as salmon","Walnuts, a plant source","Canola oil, a plant source","Flaxseed oil, a plant source"],"answer":0,"exp":"Fatty fish (salmon, tuna, mackerel, lake trout) and fish oil are direct sources of DHA and EPA per the slide, which recommends about 2 servings of fish per week. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 56 (Omega-3 Fatty Acid Sources)"},{"q":"What dietary pattern is recommended for chronic liver disease patients to increase tolerance, per lecture?","choices":["Extended fasting between three large daily meals","One very large meal eaten only at dinner","5-7 small meals with a late-night snack","Two large meals daily with strictly no snacking"],"answer":2,"exp":"5-7 small meals with a late-night snack is recommended to increase tolerance and increase protein synthesis post-prandially and overnight. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"In developing countries, protein-calorie malnutrition in children is most closely associated with which outcome?","choices":["A measurable decrease in overall infection risk","A noticeable improvement in overall immune function","Accelerated growth compared to well-nourished peers","Stunted growth and increased risk of infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"In developing countries, protein-calorie malnutrition stunts growth and increases the risk of infection. It's somewhat rare in developed countries, though it's seen in alcoholics and sometimes the elderly. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 87 (Protein-Calorie Malnutrition)"},{"q":"A pregnant patient reports craving and eating clay and ice. This behavior, known as pica, may be associated with which underlying condition per the lecture?","choices":["Vitamin A toxicity","Gestational diabetes","Folate deficiency","Iron deficiency"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture states pica may be associated with iron deficiency. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"The Food Allergen Labeling and Consumer Protection Act cited in the lecture requires manufacturers to do what?","choices":["Test every consumer for food allergies","Ban the sale of peanuts and tree nuts","Clearly identify the presence of major food allergens on product labels","Remove all allergens from processed foods"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the act requires manufacturers to clearly identify the presence of major food allergens on labels. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 666"},{"q":"Which food is specifically listed in the lecture as one to avoid for managing heartburn and peptic ulcers?","choices":["Plain white rice","Dried fruit snacks","Whole wheat bread","Onions and garlic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists onions and garlic among foods to avoid for heartburn and peptic ulcer management. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"An infant born whose fetal weight falls below the 10th percentile for gestational age would be classified as:","choices":["Macrosomic for gestational age","Classified as low birth weight","Considered a post-term infant","Small for gestational age (SGA)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture defines SGA as fetal weight below the 10th percentile for gestational age. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"According to the lecture, how can improving lifestyle influence a person's genetic risk?","choices":["It only matters after age 85","It can offset a family history or genetic predisposition to premature heart disease","It has no measurable effect at all on inherited genetic cardiac risk","It permanently rewrites a person's genes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: improving lifestyle can offset genetic predisposition to premature heart disease. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which of the following is a monitored complication specific to a nasal feeding tube, per lecture?","choices":["Nasal erosion, requiring the tube to be replaced roughly every 30 days with alternating nostrils","Noticeably improved visual acuity within the first few weeks after tube placement","A gradual improvement in the sense of smell over several weeks of tube use","Permanent, irreversible hair loss developing along the tube's nasal insertion path"],"answer":0,"exp":"Nasal erosion is a monitored tube complication; nasal tubes should be replaced roughly every 30 days, alternating nostrils, to help manage this risk. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is recommended when a person has a minor setback (a \"lapse\") in a weight-loss plan, per lecture?","choices":["Immediately restrict calories far below the original plan as a form of self-punishment for the lapse.","Wait several full weeks before even attempting to resume any of the original healthy habits.","Do not overreact, take charge immediately, and modify internal language so the lapse doesn't collapse all effort.","Give up on the entire plan permanently, since the setback proves it was never going to work."],"answer":2,"exp":"The lecture recommends not overreacting, taking charge immediately, and modifying internal language (e.g. reframing a slip positively) so that one lapse doesn't collapse all prior effort. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"The lecture cites about how many kilocalories per day for a 6-month-old child, met by breast milk or formula?","choices":["About 1,500 kilocalories per day","About 700 kilocalories per day","About 200 kilocalories per day","About 2,000 kilocalories per day"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: about 700 kilocalories per day is cited for a 6-month-old, supplied by breast milk or formula. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Which of the following is explicitly listed among the lecture's food-choice influences?","choices":["Early influences","Employer wellness policy","Genetic testing results","Local weather patterns"],"answer":0,"exp":"\"Early influences\" (paralleling early childhood experiences shaping eating habits) is explicitly listed among the lecture's food-choice influences, alongside flavor/texture/appearance, habits/availability, marketing, dining out, time, cost, sustainability, and nutrition. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 3 (Food Choice Influences)"},{"q":"What type of dietary fat is emphasized for heart failure patients, per lecture?","choices":["Saturated animal fat","Trans fat, in moderation","Fat intake is irrelevant in heart failure management.","Unsaturated fat, favoring plant over animal sources"],"answer":3,"exp":"Heart failure management emphasizes heart-healthy unsaturated fat, favoring plant sources over animal sources, to help prevent cholesterol buildup. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Per lecture, how many of the 6 ASPEN characteristics need to be present to diagnose malnutrition?","choices":["1 of 6","All 6","2 of 6","4 of 6"],"answer":2,"exp":"2 of the 6 ASPEN characteristics should be present to diagnose malnutrition. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed in the lecture as an advantage of breastfeeding specifically for the mother?","choices":["Reduced risk of obesity later in life for the child","Improved long-term cognitive performance measured in the child","Freedom from bacterial contamination in the feeding","Accelerated uterine involution due to uterine contractions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists uterine contraction and accelerated uterine involution as advantages of breastfeeding for the mother. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What is the Recommended Dietary Allowance for protein in a healthy adult, per the lecture?","choices":["8 grams per kilogram of body weight","0.08 grams per kilogram of body weight","130 grams per day regardless of weight","0.8 grams per kilogram of healthy body weight"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the Recommended Dietary Allowance is 0.8 grams of protein per kilogram of healthy body weight. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 84"},{"q":"About how many people lived in food-insecure households in the US in 2022, per lecture?","choices":["44.2 million","4.4 million","144 million","14.2 million"],"answer":0,"exp":"In 2022, 44.2 million people lived in food-insecure households in the US, per lecture. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"How does the lecture distinguish nonceliac gluten sensitivity from celiac disease?","choices":["It only affects children younger than three years old","It flattens intestinal villi in the same way celiac disease does","Symptoms occur after gluten ingestion, but there is no intestinal damage","It is caused specifically by IgE-mediated immune reactions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states nonceliac gluten sensitivity involves symptoms after gluten ingestion but no intestinal damage. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"The lecture states a diet rich in which nutrients decreases the risk of macular degeneration in aging?","choices":["Simple sugars","Sodium","Carotenoids","Saturated fats"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: a diet rich in carotenoids decreases the risk of macular degeneration. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"The lecture emphasizes that the brain grows faster during which period than at any other time?","choices":["The preschool years, ages 4 to 5","The first year of life","The teenage growth spurt","The toddler years, ages 2 to 3"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the brain grows faster during the first year than at any other time. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A patient develops temporary difficulty digesting lactose during a bout of infectious diarrhea. Per the slide's distinction, this is best classified as which type?","choices":["Lactose intolerance, present in 25% of North American adults","Congenital lactase deficiency","Primary lactose maldigestion, a normal pattern beginning at ages 3–5","Secondary lactose maldigestion, due to a specific temporary cause"],"answer":3,"exp":"Secondary lactose maldigestion is a temporary condition caused by a specific insult — like diarrhea — to the intestinal lining, distinct from primary maldigestion's normal age-related decline. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 26 (Lactose Maldigestion and Lactose Intolerance)"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe the mechanism of proton pump inhibitors (PPIs)?","choices":["They neutralize acid already present in the stomach.","They inhibit the ability of gastric cells to secrete hydrogen ions.","They block histamine receptors on acid-producing cells.","They slow gastric motility in order to reduce reflux episodes."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the lecture states PPIs 'inhibit the ability of gastric cells to secrete hydrogen ions and thus reduce acid production.' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Per the lecture's metric summary, which statement correctly compares teaspoon-equivalents of sugar and salt?","choices":["4 g of salt and 6 g of sugar are each about one teaspoon","Teaspoon-equivalents were not given for sugar or salt","4 g of sugar and 6 g of salt are each about one teaspoon","Sugar and salt both equal exactly 5 g per teaspoon"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lecture states 4 grams of sugar is about 1 teaspoon, and separately that 6 grams of salt is about 1 teaspoon — different gram weights for the same volume because the substances have different densities. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 26 (Metric System Summary (1))"},{"q":"Which specific herbs and spices does the lecture name as examples of allergenic foods?","choices":["Cinnamon, nutmeg, and clove","Coriander, paprika, and mustard","Basil, oregano, and thyme","Turmeric, cumin, and ginger"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the lecture lists coriander, paprika, and mustard under 'herbs and spices' as examples of common allergenic foods. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What alcohol-moderation limit does the lecture give for health promotion?","choices":["Up to two drinks per day for women and four for men","No more than two drinks per day for men and one drink per day for women","No more than five drinks per day for anyone","No alcohol is permitted under any circumstances"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the moderation guidance is up to two drinks per day for men and one for women. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 40"},{"q":"A woman with pre-existing diabetes is counseled to normalize her blood glucose before conception because organogenesis, the period when major fetal organs form, occurs during which weeks of embryonic development?","choices":["Weeks 1-2","Weeks 3-8","Weeks 9-12","Weeks 20-24"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture states organogenesis occurs during weeks 3-8 of embryonic development, when maternal hyperglycemia raises the risk of congenital malformations. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 6"}]};
